findings.detail:
aRunOfBURNEDEpochsStillStopsAtTheCheckpointBehindThem builds epoch 3 continuing from a burned epoch 2, so the arithmetic branch of replayAncestry is entered exactly once. The run in the name is a run of one, and the probe's repetition across a run is unpinned. SURVIVING MUTATION, MEASURED, not argued: guard the arithmetic-branch probe with a local flag so it fires only on the first arithmetic step --

    boolean probedOnce = false;   // beside atOrigin
    ...
    if (!probedOnce && chainEpoch >= 1 && crossFromCheckpoint(hops, chainEpoch, limit)) { break; }
    probedOnce = true;

-- and `./gradlew test` is BUILD SUCCESSFUL across every module. Under that mutant a run of two or more burned epochs above a checkpointed ancestor walks the ancestry unbounded again, which is the O(all commit-log entries ever written) crossing this task exists to remove. It is not a hypothetical shape: crossFromCheckpoint's own sibling comment and neverOpened's javadoc both say the arithmetic branch comes from a store outage spanning a failover, and an outage spanning two failovers burns two. The fixture cost of closing it is one epoch: burn 2 and 3, open epoch 4 from 3.
